Dear VoxelMatters Editorial Team,

Solidon3D 0.3.0 has been released. The desktop application adapts imported 3D
models, constructs printable parts from dimensions and tested components, and
prepares them for manufacturing without a cloud project platform. Its local
workflow connects three steps usually handled in isolation: adapting the
geometry, assigning the actual material configuration, and handing structured
data to the slicer.

The new release recognises existing holes in imported STL and 3MF geometry
and changes their diameter without rebuilding the model. Each modification is
stored as a reversible, parameterised operation. Users can add
standards-derived fasteners, heat-set inserts or bearing seats and drive fit
values from a printed calibration piece.

On the production side, every physical filament spool can carry its own
colour, temperatures, cooling, retraction and material values. Solidon writes
those settings to the correct material slot in 3MF and maps them into the
external slicer handoff. Multi-plate projects keep their selected plates and
spools separate.

Since the first 0.1 demo, 220 curated user-facing changes also added
in-viewport sketching, reusable parametric parts made from operation history,
printable matching fasteners and stronger feature tracking. The application
runs on Windows, macOS and Linux without an account, cloud storage or
telemetry. Its optional LLM layer may request registered operations but cannot
calculate geometry or execute generated code.

Product: https://solidon3d.de/en/
